  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nClear skies persist across the area this afternoon as high \ntemperatures have warmed to the 80s. Winds remain more on the breezy \nside, mainly for locations along and west of the James River where \ngusts up to 20-30 mph are being observed. Winds will weaken some \nthrough the evening hours but remain elevated overnight as an upper \nlevel wave begins to eject into the Northern Plains, strengthening \nthe low level jet (LLJ). The LLJ will also keep low temperatures on \nthe warm side with lows only falling to the 60s to low 70s \novernight. Patchy valley fog is possible again tonight though \nnorthwest Iowa will have the best potential for fog as winds will be \nweakest there. \n\nMonday will be a warmer day as high temperatures warm to the 80s and \n90s. With will remain breezy with gusts up to 35 mph expected during \nthe afternoon time frame. A cold front will be pushing into the area \nfrom the west by the late afternoon and early evening timeframe. \nThis front may have the potential to develop showers and \nthunderstorms. However, steep mid level lapse rates will yield a \nstout capping inversion. There also looks to be mid to high level \nclouds situated above the front throughout the day on Monday that \ncould result in slightly cooler temperatures. That said, both the \nHREF and REFS have decreased their probabilities for a strong cap to \nremain in place. They now show a 40-70% chance for convective \ninhibition (CIN) to remain at -50 J/kg or less. This suggests that \nthe cap could be weak enough for one or two storms to develop along \nthe cold front as it pushes into the Missouri River Valley. This \npotential is still uncertain as the latest 12z hi-res guidance shows \nsome variance in storms developing along the front. This may be a \nscenario where storms develop in the evening along the elevated \nportion of the cold front where CIN will be less. Should storms \ndevelop, they continue to look to be just ahead of the stronger \nvertical shear. Still not out of the question for these isolated \nstorms to be strong to severe. Effective shear values look to be on \nthe order of about 40 knots while min to max theta-e difference \nvalues will be down to about -15K. Thus, large hail up to the size \nof half dollars and damaging winds to 60 mph will be possible if the \nisolated storms do develop. Chances for strong to severe storms will \nbe waning into the overnight hours but rain showers look to persist \nthrough the night along the elevated front.\n\nThe surface front will push through the forecast area during the \novernight hours. However, the elevated front will remain over mainly \nnorthwest Iowa for the day. This is where the bulk of the rain is \nexpected to fall. The rain and cloud cover will keep high \ntemperatures on the cool side with highs only reaching up to the \n70s. The rain will persist through the entirety of Tuesday and \nthrough Wednesday morning before pushing southeast of the area. The \nensembles continue to show moderate to high probabilities between a \n50-80% chance for rainfall amounts to exceed a tenth of an inch. \nTheir probabilities decrease to 0-40% and are concentrated along and \nsouth of highway-20. Thus, light rainfall amounts of a tenth of an \ninch to possibly exceeding a quarter of an inch is most likely at \nthis time. \n\nWith lingering cloud cover around, Wednesday will be another cool \nday with highs only warming to the 70s. Broad troughing will will \nreside over the Northern Plains for the back half of the week and \nnext weekend. Temperatures look to warm back to near seasonable in \nthe 80s to potentially 90s each day. There could potentially be more \nchances for rain but these chances are uncertain for now.",
